    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d46">

                <pb facs="0062" n="740" sameAs="frus:frus1863p2/pg740"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Dayton</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 348.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-05-23"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >May</hi> 23, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of May 8th (No. 305) has been
                    received. It is proper for me to correct a misapprehension into which you seem
                    to have been led by some remarks of Mr. Drouyn de l’Huys, namely, that I had
                    suggested to Mr. Mercier, with a view to the action of the French government, a
                    blockade of Matamoras. This is erroneous. Any suggestion of that kind that may
                    have reached Mr. Drouyn de l’Huys from Mr. Mercier must have been made from
                    impressions of his own, and on his own authority, although it is not improbable
                    that he conceived the thought as the result of a free conversation with me, in
                    which I mentioned, with some earnestness, the difficulties we sustain in seeing
                    that the neutral port of Mexico is used as the entrepôt for munitions of war,
                    which, if we attempt to seize them, are covered by the pretence that they are
                    designed for another belligerent, while, if we let them pass on that ground,
                    they are received and used for our destruction. It will not be necessary for you
                    to make any explanations to Mr. Drouyn de l’Huys on the subject. Mr. Mercier
                    will doubtlessly do that.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William</hi> L. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dayton</hi>, Esq., <hi rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c</hi>., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
